values ('concepts-1-acids-bases-3', 'acids-bases', 'mcq', 'A weak acid is mostly un-ionized in pure water, but almost fully ionized when added to blood. The best explanation is that:', null, '["Blood buffers remove the released H+, pulling the dissociation forward","Blood enzymes catalyze the dissociation","Blood is colder than pure water","Blood has no water"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Blood is buffered near pH 7.4, so released H+ is taken up by buffer bases such as bicarbonate. Removing a product shifts the equilibrium HA ⇌ H+ + A− to the right (Le Châtelier). A catalyst would only speed up reaching equilibrium; it would not change where the equilibrium lies.', 3, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
