values ('concepts-1-cars-passages-4', 'cars-passages', 'mcq', 'The author''s statement that the cost ''consists of thoughts that never occurred'' suggests that the cost is:', 'We treat boredom as a defect to be repaired—an empty minute is something to fill, and a phone makes the filling effortless. Yet the empty minute once had a job. Waiting for a train or sitting through a dull lecture, the mind drifted, and in drifting it rearranged what it already held. People who report their best ideas arriving in the shower are not describing magic; they are describing one of the few remaining places where nothing else is offered.

This is not an argument that boredom is pleasant. It is not, and those who romanticize it usually have the luxury of choosing it. A factory worker repeating one motion for eight hours is bored in a way that produces nothing but fatigue. The useful kind of boredom is brief, unstructured and freely entered; the harmful kind is long, imposed and inescapable. To praise the first is not to excuse the second.

What has changed is not the amount of tedium in our lives but our tolerance for its smallest doses. We now reach for stimulation before boredom has even had a chance to begin. The cost is hard to measure, because it consists of thoughts that never occurred. But a culture that cannot sit still for two minutes should not be surprised to find that it has trouble thinking for two hours.', '["Easy to measure","Real but difficult to observe directly","Imaginary","Limited to factory workers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The author says the cost is ''hard to measure'' because you cannot count ideas that never happened, yet still treats it as a real loss.', 1, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
