  where not public.questions.reviewed;
insert into public.questions (id, topic_id, kind, prompt, passage, choices, answer_index, answer_explanation, difficulty, source_name, source_url, license)
values ('concepts-1-cars-passages-6', 'cars-passages', 'mcq', 'The author''s attitude toward the view that repairs should be visible is best described as:', 'When an old building is restored, someone must decide which past to restore it to. A church built in one century, enlarged in the next and damaged in a third carries all three histories in its stones. Stripping it back to its ''original'' form erases the later additions, which may be the parts the community actually remembers and uses. Preserving every layer, on the other hand, can leave a structure that pleases historians and confuses everyone else.

Some conservators answer that the goal is honesty: repairs should be visible, so that no visitor mistakes new work for old. Others argue that a building is not a museum label but a living place, and that a seamless repair respects the people who must use it every day. Both sides claim to serve the building; they differ about whom the building is for.

I find the second view more persuasive, though not without reservation. A building that cannot be used will not be maintained, and a building that is not maintained will not survive to be honest about anything. Still, the first view protects against a real danger: that restorers, freed from any obligation to show their work, will quietly substitute their own taste for the evidence of the past. The best restorations I know manage both—they are comfortable to use, and a careful observer can still tell where the old ends and the new begins.', '["Complete rejection","Uncritical enthusiasm","Respectful partial agreement","Indifference"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The author prefers the ''living place'' view but says the honesty view ''protects against a real danger'' and praises restorations that do both. That is qualified respect, not rejection.', 2, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
