I find the second view more persuasive, though not without reservation. A building that cannot be used will not be maintained, and a building that is not maintained will not survive to be honest about anything. Still, the first view protects against a real danger: that restorers, freed from any obligation to show their work, will quietly substitute their own taste for the evidence of the past. The best restorations I know manage both—they are comfortable to use, and a careful observer can still tell where the old ends and the new begins.', '["Construction costs","Building materials","Which audience''s needs should come first","Which historical period is most important"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The author says that both sides claim to serve the building but ''differ about whom the building is for'': visitors and historians versus the people who use it every day.', 2, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
