values ('starter-cars-passages-1', 'cars-passages', 'mcq', 'Which statement best expresses the author''s main point?', 'For most of history, maps were not neutral pictures of the land but arguments about it. A medieval mappa mundi placed Jerusalem at its center not because its makers misjudged distances, but because they were mapping meaning rather than miles. Modern readers tend to treat such maps as failed attempts at accuracy, yet this judgment assumes that accuracy was the goal. The same assumption hides the persuasive work of our own maps. A road atlas that shows highways in thick red lines and footpaths not at all tells drivers what matters, and in doing so quietly shapes which towns grow and which ones fade. The point is not that every map lies. It is that every map selects, and selection is a choice made by someone for some purpose. To read a map well, then, is to ask not only whether it is correct, but what it was made to help its users do, and what it was made to help them ignore.', '["Medieval maps were less accurate than modern maps.","Every map reflects choices about what to show, made for some purpose.","Road atlases should include footpaths.","Maps should not be trusted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The passage builds to ''every map selects, and selection is a choice made by someone for some purpose.''', 2, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
on conflict (id) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, passage = excluded.passage, choices = excluded.choices,
  answer_index = excluded.answer_index, answer_explanation = excluded.answer_explanation,
  source_name = excluded.source_name, source_url = excluded.source_url, license = excluded.license
  where not public.questions.reviewed;
insert into public.questions (id, topic_id, kind, prompt, passage, choices, answer_index, answer_explanation, difficulty, source_name, source_url, license)
values ('starter-cars-passages-2', 'cars-passages', 'mcq', 'The author mentions the road atlas mainly in order to:', 'For most of history, maps were not neutral pictures of the land but arguments about it. A medieval mappa mundi placed Jerusalem at its center not because its makers misjudged distances, but because they were mapping meaning rather than miles. Modern readers tend to treat such maps as failed attempts at accuracy, yet this judgment assumes that accuracy was the goal. The same assumption hides the persuasive work of our own maps. A road atlas that shows highways in thick red lines and footpaths not at all tells drivers what matters, and in doing so quietly shapes which towns grow and which ones fade. The point is not that every map lies. It is that every map selects, and selection is a choice made by someone for some purpose. To read a map well, then, is to ask not only whether it is correct, but what it was made to help its users do, and what it was made to help them ignore.', '["Show that modern maps can persuade, too","Criticize car culture","Prove that modern maps are inaccurate","Contrast modern accuracy with medieval error"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The atlas example follows ''The same assumption hides the persuasive work of our own maps.''', 2, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
