values ('starter-cars-passages-3', 'cars-passages', 'mcq', 'The author would most likely agree that:', 'For most of history, maps were not neutral pictures of the land but arguments about it. A medieval mappa mundi placed Jerusalem at its center not because its makers misjudged distances, but because they were mapping meaning rather than miles. Modern readers tend to treat such maps as failed attempts at accuracy, yet this judgment assumes that accuracy was the goal. The same assumption hides the persuasive work of our own maps. A road atlas that shows highways in thick red lines and footpaths not at all tells drivers what matters, and in doing so quietly shapes which towns grow and which ones fade. The point is not that every map lies. It is that every map selects, and selection is a choice made by someone for some purpose. To read a map well, then, is to ask not only whether it is correct, but what it was made to help its users do, and what it was made to help them ignore.', '["A perfectly accurate map would make no selections.","Judging old maps only by accuracy can misread their purpose.","Maps have no effect on how towns develop.","Most mapmakers intend to deceive."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The author says judging the mappa mundi as a failed attempt at accuracy ''assumes that accuracy was the goal.'' The passage also explicitly denies that every map lies.', 2, 'MedAi question bank (draft)', null, 'Original MedAi content. Draft, not yet reviewed.')
